-- title:
--   Germ ideal of a homomorphism equals ideal of its kernel
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field. Let $f_A : A \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and $f' : A' \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be schemes over $k$, each equipped with a relative group law (`RelativeGroupLaw`: functorial multiplication, unit and inverse on $T$-points over $\operatorname{Spec} k$, with associativity, unit and inverse laws and compatibility with base change). Let $F_A, F'$ be $2$-dimensional formal group laws over $k$ and let $\theta_A, \theta''$ be systems of formal coordinates, i.e. assignments sending a $k$-algebra $B'$ and a tuple $s : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to B'$ to a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} B' \to A$ (resp. $\to A'$) over $\operatorname{Spec} k$, assumed to satisfy `IsFormalCoordinates` for the respective group laws: compatibility with $k$-algebra maps on nilpotent tuples, and, for every ideal $J$ with $J^{n+1} = \bot$, the property that tuples in $J$ give infinitesimal points, that $\theta$ is injective on such tuples, that every infinitesimal point is of this form, and that $\theta$ transports the truncated formal addition into the group law. Let $p : A \to A'$ satisfy $f' \circ p = f_A$. Let $T$ be a pair of power series in two variables over $k$ which is a homomorphism $F_A \to F'$ in the sense of `IsLawHom` (vanishing constant coefficients and the substitution identity), and assume $T$ computes the germ of $p$: for every $k$-algebra $B''$, ideal $J$ with $J^{m+1} = \bot$ and tuple $s$ with entries in $J$, the morphism underlying $\theta_A(B'', s)$ followed by $p$ equals the morphism underlying $\theta''(B'', (\mathrm{nilEval}\, m\, (T_i)\, s)_i)$, where $\mathrm{nilEval}$ evaluates the degreewise truncation of a series at $s$. Let $\gamma$ be a pair of power series with zero constant coefficients, and let $\iota : K \to A$ be a morphism which is the kernel of $p$ on points, in the sense that for every morphism $t : \bullet \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every point $P$ of $A$ over $t$, the composite $P$ followed by $p$ is the unit of $L'$ at $t$ if and only if $P$ factors through $\iota$; assume also that on infinitesimal points $\iota$ is cut out by $\gamma$: for every $k$-algebra $B''$, ideal $J$ with $J^{n+1} = \bot$ and tuple $s$ in $J$, the point $\theta_A(B'', s)$ factors through $\iota$ if and only if $\mathrm{nilEval}\, n\, (\gamma_i)\, s = 0$ for all $i$. Then the ideals of $k[[X_1,X_2]]$ generated by the two components of $T$ and by the two components of $\gamma$ coincide.
--
--   This identifies the ideal generated by the germ of an isogeny-like homomorphism with the ideal cutting out its kernel on infinitesimal points, the formal counterpart of the statement $\ker \hat p = \hat K$. It feeds the construction of formal coordinates on quotients in the Čerednik–Drinfeld setting, where it is used to produce a homomorphism of formal groups realising the quotient by $K$.
